{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1082", "sample_idx": 30, "model_name": "google/gemma-3n-e2b-it", "content": "The sunlight felt…different. Not just brighter, but somehow *rendered*. Arthur blinked, the familiar floral pattern of his curtains resolving into sharp, pixelated detail. He sat up, a wave of nausea washing over him, and noticed the subtle imperfections in his room – the slightly off-center lamp, the oddly placed bookshelf – all meticulously crafted. A glance at his reflection in the antique mirror confirmed his suspicion. His clothes, usually a faded blue t-shirt and jeans, were now a ridiculously ornate tunic and leather leggings. A strange, digital hum seemed to vibrate from the very air. He reached out to touch the mirror, and his fingers passed right through. Panic flared. This wasn't real. It couldn't be. He scrambled out of bed, tripping over a plush, oversized rug that felt unnervingly springy. He looked out the window, and the world beyond wasn't his familiar suburban street, but a vibrant, stylized landscape of rolling green hills and towering, impossibly tall trees.\n\n***\n\nA small, wooden signpost stood on the lawn, displaying a crudely drawn map with symbols he didn't recognize. A cheerful, pixelated NPC – a woman with bright pink hair and a ridiculously oversized bow – skipped towards him, offering a welcoming smile. \"Welcome to Eldoria, adventurer!\" she chirped, her voice synthesized and slightly tinny. \"Are you ready to embark on your quest? The Shadow Blight threatens to consume the land, and only a hero can stop it!\" Arthur stared, speechless. This was insane. He was trapped in a video game. He had no idea what his quest was, or how to even begin. But as the pink-haired NPC continued to babble about powerful artifacts and fearsome monsters, a flicker of something else ignited within him – a strange sense of purpose, a thrilling, terrifying realization that maybe, just maybe, this wasn't a nightmare. Maybe this was an adventure.\n"